<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Everest</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sagarmatha, Chomolungma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Everest is the highest mountain on Earth, rising to about 8,849 metres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Known in Nepali as Sagarmatha and in Tibetan as Chomolungma, it was named by the British after the surveyor George Everest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Edmund Hillary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenzing-norgay">Tenzing Norgay</a> made the first confirmed ascent on 29 May 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-everest.jpg" alt="Mount Everest" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rdevany · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mt._Everest_from_Gokyo_Ri_November_5,_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Everest lies in the Mahalangur Himal sub-range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, and its summit marks part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> border. A joint survey by Nepalese and Chinese authorities in 2020 measured its height as 8,848.86 metres above sea level. Because that height is taken from the geoid, which approximates sea level, climbers ascend only part of it; the nearest sea, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, is almost 700 kilometres away. The mountain has an ice cap climate, with every month averaging well below freezing, and very little native life: a moss grows at 6,480 metres, and a small black jumping spider has been found as high as 6,700 metres.</p><p>The Nepali name is Sagarmāthā, literally 'head in the sky'. The Tibetan name Qomolangma means 'holy mother'; it was first recorded in a Chinese transcription in the 1721 Kangxi Atlas and reached the West in 1733 on a map by the French geographer D'Anville. The official Chinese form is a phonetic rendering of the Tibetan, adopted after a decree of May 1952 that chose a single name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1802 the British began the Great Trigonometrical Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to fix the positions, heights and names of the world's highest mountains. The teams, using theodolites that each weighed 500 kilograms and needed 12 men to carry, reached the Himalayan foothills by the 1830s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, suspicious of British intentions, refused their requests to enter, so the surveyors worked from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> to the south, where torrential rain and malaria killed three survey officers and forced two others to retire.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> also barred foreigners from climbing, so the first recorded attempts were British expeditions on the North Ridge from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The reconnaissance of 1921 reached 7,000 metres on the North Col. In 1922 climbers went above 8,000 metres for the first time, reaching 8,321 metres. On 8 June 1924 George Mallory and Andrew Irvine made a final summit attempt and never returned, and it remains debated whether they reached the top. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenzing-norgay">Tenzing Norgay</a>, who had reached 8,595 metres with the Swiss expedition of 1952, made the first documented ascent in 1953 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-hillary">Edmund Hillary</a> by the Southeast Ridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only about 200 people had reached the summit by 1987. Large national expeditions were the norm until commercial expeditions began in the 1990s. The mountain's dangers include winds beyond 320 kilometres an hour when the jet stream descends, blizzards and avalanches. Climbers usually start from base camps above 5,000 metres; on the Nepalese side they fly to Lukla and trek to the South Base Camp, while on the Tibetan side most drive to the North Base Camp. Nepal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> each manage climbing through permits.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Everest” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s mountains also made it a centre of world mountaineering after <b>Everest</b> was first climbed in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Two Westland aircraft of the Houston expedition flew over the summit of <b>Mount Everest</b>, crews breathing bottled oxygen in open cockpits.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
